========================================================================
  THE INTERCHANGE GRAPH AND THE META-GRAPH G_n
  opus-2026-03-22-S203
========================================================================

PART 1: THREE GRAPHS AT n=5
============================================================

  Classifying all 1024 tournaments at n=5...
  1024 tournaments → 9 score sequences → 12 iso classes


PART 2: INTERCHANGE GRAPHS I(s) — 3-CYCLE REVERSALS
============================================================

  The INTERCHANGE GRAPH I(s):
    Vertices: all tournaments with sorted score s
    Edges: pairs connected by a single 3-cycle reversal

  Brualdi-Li theorem: I(s) is REGULAR with degree = c₃(T).
  (Every tournament with score s has the same number of 3-cycles.)

  Ryser (1964): I(s) is CONNECTED.

  score      (0, 1, 2, 3, 4): size= 120, |I edges|=  180, c₃=0, regular=True, connected=False, iso=1, H=[1]
  score      (0, 1, 3, 3, 3): size=  40, |I edges|=   80, c₃=1, regular=True, connected=False, iso=1, H=[3]
  score      (0, 2, 2, 2, 4): size=  40, |I edges|=  140, c₃=1, regular=True, connected=True, iso=1, H=[3]
  score      (0, 2, 2, 3, 3): size= 120, |I edges|=  240, c₃=2, regular=True, connected=False, iso=1, H=[5]
  score      (1, 1, 1, 3, 4): size=  40, |I edges|=   80, c₃=1, regular=True, connected=False, iso=1, H=[3]
  score      (1, 1, 2, 2, 4): size= 120, |I edges|=  240, c₃=2, regular=True, connected=False, iso=1, H=[5]
  score      (1, 1, 2, 3, 3): size= 240, |I edges|=  960, c₃=3, regular=True, connected=False, iso=2, H=[9]
  score      (1, 2, 2, 2, 3): size= 280, |I edges|=  740, c₃=4, regular=False, connected=False, iso=3, H=[11, 13, 15]
  score      (2, 2, 2, 2, 2): size=  24, |I edges|=   60, c₃=5, regular=True, connected=True, iso=1, H=[15]


PART 3: INTERCHANGE vs ARC-FLIP vs META-GRAPH
============================================================

  THREE TYPES OF MOVES on tournaments:

  TYPE A: Single arc flip (hypercube edge)
    - Changes exactly 1 arc
    - May or may not change score sequence
    - May or may not change iso class

  TYPE B: 3-cycle reversal (interchange edge)
    - Changes exactly 3 arcs
    - ALWAYS preserves score sequence
    - May or may not change iso class

  TYPE C: Vertex relabeling (S_n action)
    - Changes labeling but preserves structure
    - ALWAYS preserves iso class
    - May or may not preserve score sequence

  G_n captures the QUOTIENT by Type C.
  The interchange graph captures Type B within a fiber.
  The arc-flip graph captures Type A.

  KEY QUESTION: How does the interchange graph project onto G_n?
  Within each score fiber, which interchange edges cross between
  different iso classes?

  INTERCHANGE EDGES: same iso class vs different iso class

  Score (1, 1, 2, 3, 3) (2 iso classes):
    Same-class interchange edges: 600
    Cross-class interchange edges: 360
    Cross-class H pairs: {(9, 9)}

  Score (1, 2, 2, 2, 3) (3 iso classes):
    Same-class interchange edges: 380
    Cross-class interchange edges: 360
    Cross-class H pairs: {(11, 13), (11, 15)}


PART 4: BRUALDI-LI DEGREE FORMULA
============================================================

  Brualdi-Li: The interchange graph I(s) is regular with degree:

    deg(I(s)) = c₃ = C(n,3) - Σ C(s_i, 2)

  where s = (s_1, ..., s_n) is the score sequence.
  (This is because c₃ = # cyclic triples = total - # transitive triples,
  and # transitive triples = Σ C(s_i, 2).)

  Note: s_n = (0,1,...,n-1) gives c₃ = 0 (transitive: no 3-cycles).
  And s = ((n-1)/2, ..., (n-1)/2) gives c₃ = C(n,3)/4 at large n.

  THE SCORE VARIANCE S₂ = Σ s_i² CONNECTION:
    c₃ = C(n,3) - Σ C(s_i, 2) = C(n,3) - (Σ s_i² - Σ s_i)/2
       = C(n,3) - (S₂_raw - C(n,2))/2

  where S₂_raw = Σ s_i².

  So c₃ is a LINEAR function of S₂_raw:
    c₃ = C(n,3) - S₂_raw/2 + C(n,2)/2

  And from S189-S191: H ≈ H_max + b × S₂ (also linear in S₂).

  THEREFORE: c₃ is approximately LINEAR in H.
  The interchange degree IS related to the H-value!

  Verification of c₃ = C(n,3) - Σ C(s_i, 2):
                 score   c₃ actual     formula  match  S₂_raw
       (0, 1, 2, 3, 4)           0           0      ✓      30
       (0, 1, 3, 3, 3)           1           1      ✓      28
       (0, 2, 2, 2, 4)           1           1      ✓      28
       (0, 2, 2, 3, 3)           2           2      ✓      26
       (1, 1, 1, 3, 4)           1           1      ✓      28
       (1, 1, 2, 2, 4)           2           2      ✓      26
       (1, 1, 2, 3, 3)           3           3      ✓      24
       (1, 2, 2, 2, 3)           4           4      ✓      22
       (2, 2, 2, 2, 2)           5           5      ✓      20


PART 5: THE 3-CYCLE COUNT ON G_n
============================================================

  Since c₃ depends only on the score sequence, and each iso class
  has a unique score sequence (at n ≤ 4), we might expect c₃ to be
  an invariant of G_n vertices.

  BUT: at n=5, the score (1,2,2,2,3) contains 3 iso classes
  with H = 11, 13, 15. ALL three have the SAME c₃!

  So c₃ is an invariant of the SCORE CLASS, not the iso class.
  It's a function on the Landau polytope, not on G_n directly.

  However: c₃ determines the INTERCHANGE DEGREE within the fiber.
  Higher c₃ → more 3-cycle reversals available → richer within-fiber
  structure → more iso classes possible in the fiber.

    H                 score    c₃  |Aut|   orbit
    1       (0, 1, 2, 3, 4)     0      1     120
    3       (1, 1, 1, 3, 4)     1      3      40
    3       (0, 2, 2, 2, 4)     1      3      40
    3       (0, 1, 3, 3, 3)     1      3      40
    5       (1, 1, 2, 2, 4)     2      1     120
    5       (0, 2, 2, 3, 3)     2      1     120
    9       (1, 1, 2, 3, 3)     3      1     120
    9       (1, 1, 2, 3, 3)     3      1     120
   11       (1, 2, 2, 2, 3)     4      1     120
   13       (1, 2, 2, 2, 3)     4      1     120
   15       (1, 2, 2, 2, 3)     4      3      40
   15       (2, 2, 2, 2, 2)     5      5      24

  CORRELATION between c₃ and H:
  corr(c₃, H) = 0.9754


PART 6: THE QUOTIENT INTERCHANGE GRAPH Q_I
============================================================

  Define the QUOTIENT INTERCHANGE GRAPH Q_I:
    Vertices: iso classes within a given score fiber
    Edges: iso classes connected by some 3-cycle reversal

  This is the projection of the interchange graph I(s) onto G_n.

  For score sequences with only 1 iso class: Q_I is trivial (1 vertex).
  For (1,2,2,2,3) with 3 iso classes: Q_I shows which classes
  can be reached by 3-cycle reversals.

  Score (1, 2, 2, 2, 3): 280 tournaments, 3 iso classes
    H=11: 120 tournaments
    H=15: 40 tournaments
    H=13: 120 tournaments

  Quotient interchange graph Q_I((1, 2, 2, 2, 3)):
    Vertices: 3 (iso classes at H=11, H=13, H=15)
    Edges: {(11, 13), (11, 15)}
      H=11 ↔ H=13: 480 labeled interchange transitions
      H=11 ↔ H=15: 240 labeled interchange transitions

  All possible H-pairs: {(11, 13), (13, 15), (11, 15)}
  Q_I edges: {(11, 13), (11, 15)}
  Q_I is NOT complete!


PART 7: TWO EDGE TYPES IN G_n
============================================================

  G_n has edges from ARC FLIPS (single arc reversal).
  The interchange graph has edges from 3-CYCLE FLIPS (reverse all 3 arcs).

  An arc flip changes the score sequence (usually).
  A 3-cycle flip preserves the score sequence (always).

  So in G_n:
    ARC-FLIP edges: cross between different score classes (BETWEEN fibers)
    3-CYCLE edges: stay within the same score class (WITHIN fibers)

  The arc-flip edges of G_n are the "vertical" moves (change H typically).
  The 3-cycle edges are the "horizontal" moves (change structure, not score).

  AT n=5, the only fiber with multiple iso classes is (1,2,2,2,3).
  So the only 3-cycle edges in G_5 are within this fiber.

  THE G_n DECOMPOSITION:
    G_n = (arc-flip edges across fibers) ∪ (3-cycle edges within fibers)
    = "vertical" ∪ "horizontal"

  G_5 edge analysis:
    Arc-flip edges (between iso classes): 30
    3-cycle edges (between iso classes): 18
    Overlap (edges reachable by BOTH): 10
    Total G_5 edges (arc-flip only): 30 (from S201)

  3-cycle edges in G_5 (changing iso class within same score):
    H=1 ↔ H=11  (scores (0, 1, 2, 3, 4) and (1, 2, 2, 2, 3))
    H=1 ↔ H=5  (scores (0, 1, 2, 3, 4) and (0, 2, 2, 3, 3))
    H=3 ↔ H=9  (scores (0, 1, 3, 3, 3) and (1, 1, 2, 3, 3))
    H=9 ↔ H=9  (scores (1, 1, 2, 3, 3) and (1, 1, 2, 3, 3))
    H=9 ↔ H=3  (scores (1, 1, 2, 3, 3) and (1, 1, 1, 3, 4))
    H=5 ↔ H=13  (scores (1, 1, 2, 2, 4) and (1, 2, 2, 2, 3))
    H=9 ↔ H=3  (scores (1, 1, 2, 3, 3) and (1, 1, 1, 3, 4))
    H=5 ↔ H=13  (scores (0, 2, 2, 3, 3) and (1, 2, 2, 2, 3))
    H=11 ↔ H=13  (scores (1, 2, 2, 2, 3) and (1, 2, 2, 2, 3))
    H=5 ↔ H=11  (scores (1, 1, 2, 2, 4) and (1, 2, 2, 2, 3))
    H=5 ↔ H=15  (scores (0, 2, 2, 3, 3) and (1, 2, 2, 2, 3))
    H=5 ↔ H=15  (scores (1, 1, 2, 2, 4) and (1, 2, 2, 2, 3))
    H=5 ↔ H=11  (scores (0, 2, 2, 3, 3) and (1, 2, 2, 2, 3))
    H=1 ↔ H=13  (scores (0, 1, 2, 3, 4) and (1, 2, 2, 2, 3))
    H=3 ↔ H=9  (scores (0, 1, 3, 3, 3) and (1, 1, 2, 3, 3))
    H=3 ↔ H=15  (scores (0, 2, 2, 2, 4) and (2, 2, 2, 2, 2))
    H=11 ↔ H=15  (scores (1, 2, 2, 2, 3) and (1, 2, 2, 2, 3))
    H=1 ↔ H=5  (scores (0, 1, 2, 3, 4) and (1, 1, 2, 2, 4))

  Are 3-cycle edges ⊂ arc-flip edges? False
  NEW edges from 3-cycle flips (not in arc-flip G_5): 8
    H=3 ↔ H=9
    H=1 ↔ H=11
    H=9 ↔ H=3
    H=5 ↔ H=15
    H=5 ↔ H=15
    H=1 ↔ H=13
    H=11 ↔ H=15
    H=3 ↔ H=15


PART 8: THE ENRICHED META-GRAPH G_n^+
============================================================

  Define G_n^+ as G_n with BOTH types of edges:
    - Arc-flip edges (colored RED): single arc reversal
    - 3-cycle edges (colored BLUE): cyclic triple reversal

  At n=5:
    G_5 has 30 red edges (arc flips).
    How many blue edges does 3-cycle reversal add?

  G_5^+ edge breakdown:
    Red only (arc flip): 20
    Blue only (3-cycle): 8
    Both (arc AND 3-cycle): 10
    Total: 38


